# Igniton rebuild — read before changing anything

This is the **ground-up Dawn 15.4.1 rebuild**, not the theme currently serving
igniton.com. See §5 if you are unsure which repo you are in — it is easy to
get wrong, and the two have different rules.

## 1. `docs/` is the source of truth

From `docs/README.md`: *"nothing ships to the theme without a decision
recorded here first."* That is the governing rule of this repo. Read in this
order before your first task:

1. `docs/README.md` — orientation
2. `docs/brand.md` — who Igniton is, how the site should feel
3. `docs/design-system.md` — tokens, type scale, motion rules every section obeys
4. `docs/sections.md` — what is being built, in what order
5. `docs/workflow.md` — conventions, and its "What NOT to do" list
6. `docs/section-schema-standard.md` — before writing any schema

If a change contradicts the docs, update the doc in the same commit or don't
make the change. Don't let code and docs drift — the whole point of this
rebuild was escaping a theme where they had.

## 2. Every section follows the Section Schema Standard

`docs/section-schema-standard.md` defines seven canonical control groups
(colour scheme, container width, spacing, label, heading, body, image) with
copy-paste blocks and a CSS variable contract. New sections adopt it; existing
ones are being retrofitted. Don't invent a bespoke schema shape — copy the
canonical blocks and consume the CSS variable contract in §4–5 of that doc.

## 3. Never write `"default": ""`

Shopify rejects the file at sync time with
`Invalid schema: setting with id="X" default can't be blank`, then **deploys
every other file in the commit and reports success**. The theme keeps serving
the previous version of that one file, indefinitely and silently.

To make a setting optional, **omit the `default` key entirely**. It behaves
identically and validates.

- `shopify theme check` does **not** catch this — zero offenses on a file
  Shopify is actively rejecting.
- The only place the real error appears is **Shopify admin → Online Store →
  Themes → GitHub connection activity log**.
- This cost four weeks on the live theme's memory-quiz page in Sept 2026, and
  one instance was found lurking here in `product-overview.liquid`.

If a rendered page doesn't match the repo but other files deploy fine, check
that log before anything else.

## 4. House conventions

- Fonts: use `var(--font-heading-family)` / `var(--font-body-family)`. Never
  hardcode a `font-family` stack.
- No inline `<style>` blocks in sections — use the section's CSS file.
- No new colour values — add a token to `docs/design-system.md` first.
- Shopify auto-commits admin edits back to `main` as "Update from Shopify
  for theme ...", so **always pull, not just fetch**, before pushing.
- Commit only the files for your task; the working tree often carries
  unrelated in-progress work.

## 5. Which repo am I in? (check before every task)

| Repo | Path | Serves igniton.com? |
|---|---|---|
| **This one** — Dawn rebuild | `Igniton-New-Shopify` | **No** |
| Live theme | `Shopify-Igniton-Stripped-NEW-Setup` | **Yes** — `ig-*` prod sections live there |
| Reward backend | `igniton-review-reward` | Vercel fn, Recharge discounts |

The live theme is the one where a push reaches customers in about a minute.
It has its own `.cursor/rules/igniton.mdc`, plus `README.md` and a 500-line
`DEVELOPER.md`.

**A push here does deploy somewhere.** This repo is connected to a Shopify
theme called **`Igniton-New-Shopify/main`** — Shopify auto-commits admin edits
back to this branch constantly (25 such commits landed in one recent stretch),
which is why you must always **pull before pushing** or you will clobber
someone's customizer work.

That theme is not the one serving igniton.com — theme 41, from the
`Shopify-Igniton-Stripped-NEW-Setup` repo, is. So `docs/workflow.md`'s line that `main`
here "auto-deploys to the live theme" appears stale from kickoff. Treat a push
here as deploying to a real but unpublished theme, and confirm with Craig
before assuming it is invisible to customers.
